00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:29,440
Language takeaway.

35
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
All right, the first word is a very common word especially if you're a church-going person.

36
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:37,000
The word is altar.

37
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Okay, an altar.

38
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:39,600
An altar, it's a thing.

39
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:44,080
It's a thing and an altar is usually found inside a church as you said, right?

40
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:45,600
That's right.

41
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:46,600
So it's like a...

42
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:47,040
Table.

43
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:48,100
It's like a table.

44
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:48,840
Or a counter.

45
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:54,460
So the altar in the church is where the priest does all of the stuff that he does during the mass.

46
00:02:55,060 --> 00:02:58,800
It's where he prepares the bread and the wine and it's essentially a table, yeah.

47
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:04,040
But you can build an altar for yourself in your garden, for example, right?

48
00:03:04,040 --> 00:03:06,960
And it doesn't have to do with the Catholic religion necessarily.

49
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:07,470
No, not necessarily.

50
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:13,880
You could have an altar in a Greek pagan temple where you give offerings to a god or goddess.

51
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:20,790
So in this case, an altar is a special religious table where you perform certain religious rites or functions.

52
00:03:21,090 --> 00:03:22,110
Okay, very interesting.

53
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:29,760
All right, and so it's common in Mexico for people to build private altars honoring the deceased.

54
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:31,110
So what is this word, deceased?

55
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:33,880
All right, deceased is a noun.

56
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:38,250
It's a word that we use to call someone who's dead or departed.

57
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:42,200
So the deceased was named John Smith.

58
00:03:42,210 --> 00:03:45,090
So it's like a more technical term of saying somebody is dead.

59
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:50,320
Right, and it's something that you'll often read in the newspaper when they don't want to say the dead guy.

60
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:56,180
The deceased was a doctor and he is survived by three children.

61
00:03:56,440 --> 00:04:00,440
And so deceased is a way to describe a person in a nicer way.

62
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:01,600
Right, very good, the deceased.

63
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:05,840
And you mentioned another word that's very closely related to deceased, the departed.

64
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:11,500
That's right, and this is actually a famous word because of a Martin Scorsese movie that came out about two years ago.

65
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:15,800
And departed, you know the word to depart, to leave.

66
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:20,440
A person that is a departed is someone who has left us.

67
00:04:22,540 --> 00:04:23,000
It's a dead person.

68
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:28,700
So you can say the deceased or the departed, which basically means the same thing as the dead guy or the dead person.

69
00:04:28,940 --> 00:04:30,860
Exactly, but again, it's much more polite.

70
00:04:31,220 --> 00:04:34,360
It's something that we hear often at funerals or that we read in magazines.

71
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:39,320
Okay, so for example, All Saints Day is a way of honoring the departed.

72
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:41,030
Exactly, very nice sounding.

73
00:04:41,270 --> 00:04:42,170
Yeah, it sounds better.

74
00:04:42,670 --> 00:04:54,050
All right, so talking a little bit more about the departed and the traditions, it says here that many Mexicans use sugar skulls or have sugar skulls during this festivity.

75
00:04:54,520 --> 00:04:55,520
That's right.

76
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:57,870
So a skull is the bones from a person's head.

77
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:03,970
And so you can often see these on, for example, pirate ships.

78
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:06,360
We say skull and crossbones.

79
00:05:06,360 --> 00:05:10,640
And so it's the bones from a head and you have the bones underneath.

80
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:13,370
It's a very popular image on Halloween because it's scary.

81
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:17,360
But a skull is essentially the bones from a head.

82
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:21,170
So it's like a person's head without the skin, eyes, lips, everything.

83
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:28,280
Right, it's scary because it has empty black spaces where the eyes are supposed to be and you can see all the teeth, but nothing else.

84
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:29,020
What about the sugar skulls?

85
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:29,580
What are these?

86
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:30,760
They're skulls made of sugar.

87
00:05:31,520 --> 00:05:31,680
That's right.

88
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:43,480
And so they look like skulls because there's no cheeks and there's no skin or eyes, but they're beautiful because the Mexicans in their tradition, they color the sugar.

89
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:51,490
So you can have white skulls with this beautiful green and pink and red and blue decorations and you can eat them because they're made of sugar.

90
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:57,440
And so it's a special treat that people have in the Mexican tradition on the Day of the Dead.

91
00:05:57,440 --> 00:05:58,440
Very good.

92
00:05:58,630 --> 00:06:06,110
And one of the many traditions apart from sugar skulls and building private altars, they also visit the person's tomb, if possible, right?

